    "lem-fourth-moment-quadratic-form-resolvent-bound": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be random elements with the same measurable target $(E,\\mathcal E)$. They are **identically distributed** if $\\mathbb P(X_i\\in B)=\\mathbb P(X_j\\in B)$ for all $i,j\\in I$ and $B\\in\\mathcal E$, that is, their laws in Law or distribution of a random element agree. They are **independent and identically distributed (IID)** if, in addition, the whole family is independent in Independent random elements. Independence means mutual independence, not merely pairwise independence. No moment assumption is part of either definition. The empty family satisfies these universal conditions vacuously.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-independent-random-elements-have-product-joint-law",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, and let\n$X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ for $i<n$ be independent\nrandom elements. Define\n\n$$X=(X_0,\\dots,X_{n-1}):\\Omega\\to\\prod_{i<n}S_i.$$\n\nThen $X$ is a random element of\n$\\left(\\prod_{i<n}S_i,\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\Sigma_i\\right)$, and its law is the\nfinite product of the marginal laws:\n\n$$\\mathbb P_X=\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\mathbb P_{X_i}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "First fix a deterministic complex symmetric matrix $A$. Since $A_{ji}=A_{ij}$, $$\\xi^{\\mathsf T}A\\xi-\\operatorname{tr}A =\\sum_i A_{ii}(\\xi_i^2-1)+2\\sum_{i<j}A_{ij}\\xi_i\\xi_j.$$ The diagonal summands have mean zero. Distinct diagonal summands are uncorrelated; a diagonal summand and an off-diagonal summand are uncorrelated because at least one independent centered coordinate occurs to the first power; and two off-diagonal summands are uncorrelated unless their unordered index pairs coincide. Therefore $$\\mathbb E\\left|\\xi^{\\mathsf T}A\\xi-\\operatorname{tr}A\\right|^2 =(\\mu_4-1)\\sum_i|A_{ii}|^2+4\\sum_{i<j}|A_{ij}|^2 \\le C_{\\mu_4}\\sum_{i,j}|A_{ij}|^2.$$ Here $\\mu_4\\ge1$ follows from $\\mathbb E\\xi_i^2=1$ and Cauchy–Schwarz.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "By [F2], the joint law of $(\\xi,A)$ is the product of its marginal laws. The integrand is integrable: $|\\xi^{\\mathsf T}A\\xi-\\operatorname{tr}A|^2\\le2\\|A\\|_F^2\\|\\xi\\|_2^4+2d\\|A\\|_F^2$, and $\\mathbb E\\|\\xi\\|_2^4<\\infty$ because $d$ is finite and $\\mathbb E\\xi_i^4<\\infty$. Thus [F3] permits integration of the deterministic inequality in step 1.1 against the law of $A$, giving the asserted bound.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
