    "thm-bai-yin-smallest-singular-value-limits": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-sample-covariance-spectrum-confinement",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\xi_{ij})_{i,j\\ge1}$ be one infinite real iid array with $E\\xi_{11}=0$, $E\\xi_{11}^2=1$ and $E|\\xi_{11}|^4<\\infty$. Let $X_h=(\\xi_{ij})_{i\\le h,j\\le p_h}$ with $p_h/h\\to\\gamma\\in(0,\\infty)$, $N_h=\\max(h,p_h)$ and $m_h=\\min(h,p_h)$. Orient $X_h$ or its transpose as a matrix $Z_h$ with $m_h$ rows and $N_h$ columns, and put $A_h=Z_h/\\sqrt{N_h}$ and $y=\\min(\\gamma,\\gamma^{-1})\\in(0,1]$. Then almost surely\n$$\\limsup_h\\left\\|A_hA_h^{\\mathsf T}-(1+y)I_{m_h}\\right\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le2\\sqrt y.$$\nNo weak convergence of empirical spectral measures is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-nonbacktracking-covariance-high-moment-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\xi_{ij})_{i,j\\ge1}$ be one infinite real iid array with $E\\xi_{11}=0$, $E\\xi_{11}^2=1$ and $E|\\xi_{11}|^4<\\infty$, and let $p_h/h\\to\\gamma\\in(0,\\infty)$. Put $N_h=\\max(h,p_h)$, $m_h=\\min(h,p_h)$ and $y_h=m_h/N_h\\to y:=\\min(\\gamma,\\gamma^{-1})$. Take the centered slow truncations of the upper-left $h\\times p_h$ blocks, orient each with $m_h$ rows and $N_h$ columns, and divide by $\\sqrt{N_h}$, obtaining $Y_h$. For each fixed integer $\\ell\\ge1$, define $T_{h,\\ell}$ by summing the products of the entries of $Y_h,Y_h^{\\mathsf T},\\ldots,Y_h,Y_h^{\\mathsf T}$ along all paths of length $2\\ell$, subject to every pair of vertices two positions apart being unequal. Its row and column endpoints are included in these exclusions. Then almost surely\n$$\\limsup_h\\|T_{h,\\ell}\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le(2\\ell+1)(\\ell+1)y^{(\\ell-1)/2}.$$\nIn particular, for the original untruncated $X_h=(\\xi_{ij})_{i\\le h,j\\le p_h}$, almost surely $\\limsup_h\\|X_h/\\sqrt h\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}<\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-fixed-degree-covariance-trace-and-edge-witnesses",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\xi_{ij})_{i,j\\ge1}$ be one infinite real iid array with $E\\xi_{11}=0$, $E\\xi_{11}^2=1$ and $E|\\xi_{11}|^2<\\infty$. Let $p_n/n\\to\\gamma\\in(0,\\infty)$, $X_n=(\\xi_{ij})_{i\\le n,j\\le p_n}$ and $G_n=X_nX_n^{\\mathsf T}/n$. Suppose almost surely $\\limsup_n\\|X_n/\\sqrt n\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}<\\infty$. For every fixed integer $k\\ge1$, almost surely\n$$\\frac1n\\operatorname{Tr}G_n^k\\longrightarrow\\mu_k(\\gamma):=\\sum_{s=1}^k\\frac1k\\binom{k}{s}\\binom{k}{s-1}\\gamma^s.$$\nPositive eigenvalues exist eventually and, with $a=(1-\\sqrt\\gamma)^2$ and $b=(1+\\sqrt\\gamma)^2$, almost surely\n$$\\liminf_n\\lambda_{\\max}(G_n)\\ge b,\\qquad\\limsup_n\\lambda_{\\min}^{+}(G_n)\\le a.$$\nThe edge conclusions follow from fixed polynomial trace witnesses and do not assume a weak empirical spectral limit.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-singular-value-decomposition",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $T:V\\to W$ be a linear map between finite-dimensional real or complex inner\nproduct spaces, and let $r$ be the number of positive singular values of $T$.\nThen there exist orthonormal bases $(e_1,\\dots,e_n)$ of $V$ and\n$(f_1,\\dots,f_m)$ of $W$, together with singular values\n\n$$s_1\\ge\\cdots\\ge s_r>0,$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$Tv=\\sum_{j=1}^r s_j\\langle v,e_j\\rangle f_j$$\n\nfor every $v\\in V$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-rank-equals-number-of-nonzero-singular-values",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $T:V\\to W$ be a linear map between finite-dimensional real or complex inner\nproduct spaces. Then $\\operatorname{rank} T$ equals the number of positive\nsingular values of $T$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Write $N_n=\\max(n,p_n)$, $r_n=\\min(n,p_n)$, $c_n=N_n/n\\to c=\\max(1,\\gamma)$ and $y_n=r_n/N_n\\to y:=\\min(\\gamma,\\gamma^{-1})$. Orient $X_n$ as $Z_n$ with $r_n$ rows. F1 and the quadratic-form lower bound give $\\liminf\\lambda_{\\min}(Z_nZ_n^{\\mathsf T}/N_n)\\ge(1-\\sqrt y)^2$ almost surely. By F3, $s_{r_n}(X_n)^2/n=c_n\\lambda_{\\min}(Z_nZ_n^{\\mathsf T}/N_n)$, so its liminf is at least $c(1-\\sqrt y)^2=(1-\\sqrt\\gamma)^2$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-2",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "If $\\gamma\\ne1$, this last constant is positive. Thus all $r_n$ singular values are positive eventually, and F3 gives eventual full column rank when $\\gamma<1$ and full row rank when $\\gamma>1$. The smallest positive eigenvalue of $X_nX_n^{\\mathsf T}/n$ then equals $s_{r_n}(X_n)^2/n$. Its limsup is at most $(1-\\sqrt\\gamma)^2$ by F2; combined with step 1.1 this proves convergence of that squared singular value. Taking the nonnegative square root gives respectively $1-\\sqrt\\gamma$ and $\\sqrt\\gamma-1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-3",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "If $\\gamma=1$, F2 supplies positive eigenvalues eventually and a smallest positive eigenvalue with limsup at most zero. Nonnegativity makes its limit zero. F3 transfers this to the square of the smallest positive singular value divided by $n$, and the nonnegative square root tends to zero. No claim of eventual full rank at aspect one is needed. The argument for either endpoint uses confinement and independent trace witnesses, not MP weak convergence.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
