    "lem-adapted-resolvent-trace-concentration": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-rank-one-resolvent-update-and-trace-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $B$ be a real symmetric non-negative $d\\times d$ matrix, let $x\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and let $z=u+i\\eta$ with $u\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\eta>0$. Put $R=(B-zI)^{-1}$ and $R_x=(B+xx^{\\mathsf T}-zI)^{-1}$. Then $1+x^{\\mathsf T}Rx\\ne0$,\n$$R_x=R-\\frac{Rxx^{\\mathsf T}R}{1+x^{\\mathsf T}Rx},$$\nand\n$$\\left|\\operatorname{tr}R_x-\\operatorname{tr}R\\right|\\le\\frac1\\eta.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-mcdiarmid-bounded-differences",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $n\\ge1$, let $Z_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ be independent random elements, meaning measurable maps (Random elements and real random variables), with laws $\\mu_i$ given by pushforward (Law or distribution of a random element). Put $\\mu=\\bigotimes_{i=1}^n\\mu_i$ on the finite product sigma-algebra (The product sigma-algebra and its finite iterates). Let $f:\\prod_{i=1}^nS_i\\to\\mathbb R$ be product-measurable with $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$. Suppose finite deterministic constants $c_i\\ge0$ satisfy\n$$|f(z_1,\\ldots,z_i,\\ldots,z_n)-f(z_1,\\ldots,z_i',\\ldots,z_n)|\\le c_i$$\nfor every fixed setting of the other coordinates and every $z_i,z_i'$. The independent product-law theorem identifies the law of $Z=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n)$ as $\\mu$. Applying change of variables for expectation first to $|f|$ and then to $f$ makes $f(Z)$ integrable and gives $\\mathbb E f(Z)=\\int f\\,d\\mu$. Put $V=\\sum_{i=1}^n c_i^2$. For $t>0$, if $V>0$ then\n$$\\mathbb P\\bigl(f(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n)-\\mathbb E f(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n)\\ge t\\bigr)\\le\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{2t^2}{V}\\right),$$\nand if $V=0$ this probability is $0$. The same bound holds for the lower tail, so the two-sided probability is at most $2e^{-2t^2/V}$ when $V>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-first-borel-cantelli-lemma-for-events",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(A_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ be events in a probability space. If\n$$\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\mathbb P(A_n)<+\\infty,$$\nthen\n$$\\mathbb P(A_n\\ \\mathrm{i.o.})=0.$$\n\nNo independence hypothesis is needed.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Fix all vectors except $Y_{n,j}$. Replacing that vector by another vector $Y'_{n,j}$ changes $B_n$ by removing $Y_{n,j}Y_{n,j}^{\\mathsf T}$ and adding $Y'_{n,j}(Y'_{n,j})^{\\mathsf T}$. Applying [F1] once to remove the first term and once to add the second shows that $\\operatorname{tr}(B_n-zI)^{-1}$ changes by at most $2/\\eta$. Hence each of the real and imaginary parts of $M_n(z)$ has coordinate oscillation at most $c_j=2/(d_n\\eta)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1-2",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Apply [F2] separately to $\\operatorname{Re}M_n(z)$ and $\\operatorname{Im}M_n(z)$. If a complex number has modulus greater than $t$, at least one of its real or imaginary parts has magnitude greater than $t/\\sqrt2$. The union bound therefore gives $$\\Pr\\{|M_n(z)-\\mathbb E M_n(z)|>t\\} \\le4\\exp\\left(-\\frac{t^2d_n^2\\eta^2}{4n}\\right).$$ This is the stated estimate.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Since $d_n/n\\to c>0$, for each fixed $t>0$ the exponent in step 2.1 is at most $-c_tn$ for all sufficiently large $n$, for some $c_t>0$. The probabilities are therefore summable.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2-2",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "Apply [F3] to the events in step 3.1 for each $t=1/k$, $k\\ge1$, and intersect the resulting countable collection of probability-one events. On that event $M_n(z)-\\mathbb E M_n(z)\\to0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 3.1",
            "choose",
            "common probability space"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The adapted concentration lemma exposes independent rank-one vectors in positive dimension and bounds both parts of the normalized complex trace. The number of independent vectors is a positive integer and the normalized matrix dimension is positive, so neither trace normalization is empty.",
          "template_review": {
            "upheld": true,
            "by": "/root/step2_reverse68",
            "reason": "The adapted concentration lemma exposes independent rank-one vectors in positive dimension and bounds both parts of the normalized complex trace. The number of independent vectors is a positive integer and the normalized matrix dimension is positive, so neither trace normalization is empty.",
            "item_sha256": "99370e8c6550951fb54433305795f0e20782b6fd28da2c8f8e5c2c816df447e7",
            "row_sha256": "2465d950a765bc444e85af897a667e145567f30a1efa9c32b0a77826d29869e4"
          },
          "reviewed": {
            "upheld": true,
            "by": "/root/step2_spectral21_repair",
            "reason": "After the guarded source repair in this packet, the Statement and Given explicitly require each d_n to be a positive integer, in addition to positive n. Thus the normalized trace 1/d_n and step 1.1 oscillation 2/(d_n eta) are defined for every n; step 2.1 retains its full finite-n tail, and steps 3.1–4.1 retain the positive-aspect almost-sure limit. The unrepaired original lacked this premise: d_1=0 and d_n=n for n>=2 would meet the limit while leaving M_1 undefined. This uphold applies only after that source repair."
          }
        },
        {
