    "lem-marchenko-pastur-formula-defines-a-finite-measure": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-marchenko-pastur-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice.\n\nFor $\\gamma>0$, set\n$$a_\\gamma=(1-\\sqrt\\gamma)^2,\\qquad b_\\gamma=(1+\\sqrt\\gamma)^2,\\qquad r_+:=\\max\\{r,0\\}\\quad(r\\in\\mathbb R).$$\nDefine the Marchenko–Pastur set function on Borel $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ by\n$$\\mu_\\gamma(A):=\\int_{A\\cap(a_\\gamma,b_\\gamma)}\\frac{\\sqrt{(b_\\gamma-x)(x-a_\\gamma)}}{2\\pi\\gamma x}\\,d\\lambda(x)+\\left(1-\\frac1\\gamma\\right)_+\\delta_0(A),$$\nwhere $\\lambda$ is Lebesgue measure on $\\mathbb R$. The formula defines a finite Borel measure; its total mass is established by the Marchenko–Pastur law below.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-identity-theorem-holomorphic-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If two holomorphic functions on a complex domain agree on a set with an accumulation point in the domain, then they agree everywhere on the domain.\n\nPrecisely, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb C$ be a complex domain (A complex domain is a nonempty connected open subset of $\\mathbb C$), let $f,g:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb C$ be holomorphic, and suppose that some $a\\in\\Omega$ is an accumulation point of $\\{z\\in\\Omega:f(z)=g(z)\\}$. Then $f=g$ on $\\Omega$. The requirement $a\\in\\Omega$ is essential.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. All functions below have domain $X$. Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-indefinite-integral-of-a-nonnegative-function-is-a-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. Let $f:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be measurable and define\n$$\\nu_f(A):=\\int_A f\\,d\\mu \\qquad (A\\in\\mathcal A).$$\nThen $\\nu_f$ is a measure on $(X,\\mathcal A)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets (Finite, countably infinite, countable, uncountable) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-lebesgue-measure-is-a-complete-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\ge 1$ and **assume the Axiom of Countable Choice**\n(The Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$)). Then:\n\n1. $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$ is a sigma-algebra on $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and\n   $\\lambda_n$ is a measure on it (Measures on sigma-algebras);\n2. the measure space $(\\mathbb{R}^n, \\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n), \\lambda_n)$ is\n   complete (Complete measure spaces), and every $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$\n   with $\\lambda_n^*(S) = 0$ is Lebesgue measurable with $\\lambda_n(S) = 0$;\n3. every elementary set is Lebesgue measurable and\n   $\\lambda_n(A) = \\mu_0(A)$ for every $A \\in \\mathcal{E}_n$; in particular\n   $\\lambda_n(B) = \\operatorname{vol}(B)$ for every half-open box $B$,\n   $\\lambda_n(\\varnothing) = 0$ and $\\lambda_n(\\mathbb{R}^n) = +\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-borel-sets-are-lebesgue-measurable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\ge 1$ and **assume the Axiom of Countable Choice**\n(The Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$)). Then\n\n$$\\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^n) \\;\\subseteq\\; \\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n) :$$\n\nevery Borel subset of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ (The Borel sigma-algebra of a topological space) is Lebesgue\nmeasurable (Lebesgue measurable sets, the family $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$, and the restricted set function $\\lambda_n$). In\nparticular every open set, every closed set and every countable intersection of\nopen sets is Lebesgue measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Put $B=2\\sqrt\\gamma$, $A_0=1+\\gamma$, and $x(\\theta)=A_0+B\\cos\\theta$ for $0<\\theta<\\pi$. This substitution maps $(0,\\pi)$ onto $(a,b)$ in reverse order, with $\\sqrt{(b-x)(x-a)}=B\\sin\\theta$ and $|dx|=B\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta$. Thus the continuous part of $\\mu_\\gamma$ becomes $(2/\\pi)\\sin^2\\theta\\,d\\theta/x(\\theta)$. For real $A>B$, the substitution $t=\\tan(\\theta/2)$ gives $$\\int_0^\\pi\\frac{d\\theta}{A+B\\cos\\theta}=2\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{dt}{(A+B)+(A-B)t^2}=\\frac{\\pi}{\\sqrt{A^2-B^2}}.$$ Write $I_0(A)=\\int_0^\\pi(A+B\\cos\\theta)^{-1}d\\theta=\\pi/\\sqrt{A^2-B^2}$. Since $\\int_0^\\pi\\cos\\theta\\,d\\theta=0$ and $\\cos\\theta/(A+B\\cos\\theta)=B^{-1}-A/[B(A+B\\cos\\theta)]$, $$I_1(A):=\\int_0^\\pi\\frac{\\cos\\theta}{A+B\\cos\\theta}d\\theta=\\frac\\pi B-\\frac A B I_0(A),\\quad I_2(A):=\\int_0^\\pi\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{A+B\\cos\\theta}d\\theta=-\\frac A B I_1(A)=-\\frac{A\\pi}{B^2}+\\frac{A^2}{B^2}I_0(A).$$ Therefore $$J(A):=\\int_0^\\pi\\frac{\\sin^2\\theta}{A+B\\cos\\theta}\\,d\\theta=I_0(A)-I_2(A)=\\frac{\\pi(A-\\sqrt{A^2-B^2})}{B^2}.$$ For $A=A_0>B$, the root is $|1-\\gamma|$; if $\\gamma=1$, direct cancellation $\\sin^2\\theta/(2+2\\cos\\theta)= (1-\\cos\\theta)/2$ gives the same value by continuity. Hence the continuous mass is $2J(A_0)/\\pi=(A_0-|1-\\gamma|)/(2\\gamma)=\\min(1,\\gamma)/\\gamma$. Adding $(1-1/\\gamma)_+$ gives total mass one. Define $f_\\gamma(x):=\\sqrt{(b-x)(x-a)}/(2\\pi\\gamma x)$ for $a<x<b$ and $f_\\gamma(x):=0$ otherwise. This is nonnegative and Borel measurable, and has finite integral by the calculation above. By [F5], $f_\\gamma$ is Lebesgue measurable. By [F4], $A\\mapsto\\int_A f_\\gamma\\,d\\lambda$ is a finite Borel measure; the nonnegative multiple $(1-1/\\gamma)_+\\delta_0$ is a finite measure as well. Their sum is the displayed set function, so it is a Borel probability measure supported on $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "algebra",
            "construct",
            "A1",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "For $w=re^{i\\theta}\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus(-\\infty,0]$, with $r>0$ and $-\\pi<\\theta<\\pi$, define $q_0(w)=\\sqrt r\\,e^{i\\theta/2}$. This is continuous, satisfies $q_0(w)^2=w$, and is holomorphic: for $h\\to0$ with $w+h$ in the slit plane, $$\\frac{q_0(w+h)-q_0(w)}h=\\frac1{q_0(w+h)+q_0(w)}\\longrightarrow\\frac1{2q_0(w)}.$$ Set $\\Omega=\\mathbb C\\setminus(-\\infty,B]$ and $q(A)=q_0(A-B)q_0(A+B)$ for $A\\in\\Omega$. Both factors are defined and holomorphic there, $q(A)^2=A^2-B^2$, and $q(A)>0$ for real $A>B$. The domain is connected: each $A-B=re^{i\\theta}$ with $-\\pi<\\theta<\\pi$ can be joined inside it to $B+1$ by continuously changing $r$ to $1$ and $\\theta$ to $0$. The integral $J(A)$ is holomorphic on $\\Omega$. Indeed, on any compact subset its denominator is bounded away from zero, and the difference quotient of its integrand tends pointwise to $-\\sin^2\\theta/(A+B\\cos\\theta)^2$ with a uniform integrable bound; [F3] justifies passing that limit through the integral. The identity theorem [F2] extends the formula from real $A>B$ (which has limit points inside $\\Omega$) to all $A\\in\\Omega$. In particular it applies to $A_z=A_0-z$, whose imaginary part is negative.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2-2",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Let $q=\\sqrt{(1+\\gamma-z)^2-4\\gamma}$ on that branch. The substitution in step 1.1 and $1/[x(x-z)]=z^{-1}((x-z)^{-1}-x^{-1})$ give the transform of the continuous part as $[(A_z-q)-(A_0-|1-\\gamma|)]/(2\\gamma z)$. Adding the atom contributes $-(1-1/\\gamma)_+/z$; simplifying in the cases $\\gamma\\le1$ and $\\gamma>1$ gives $$m_\\gamma(z)=\\frac{1-\\gamma-z-q}{2\\gamma z}.$$ Since $q^2=(1-\\gamma-z)^2-4\\gamma z$, substitution verifies $\\gamma z m_\\gamma(z)^2+(z+\\gamma-1)m_\\gamma(z)+1=0$, Since step 1.1 gives a probability measure, $\\operatorname{Im}m_\\gamma(z)=\\int\\eta/|x-z|^2\\,d\\mu_\\gamma(x)>0$. Thus $1+\\gamma m_\\gamma(z)\\ne0$, and rearranging the quadratic gives the fixed-point equation in the Statement.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ],
          "notes": "Checked mass integral, including cancellation at gamma=1, Borel density construction under countable choice, connected slit-plane square-root branch and holomorphic parameter integral, both atom cases and quadratic algebra. Confirmed refuter nonfatal omission: strict positivity of Im m now explicitly licenses division by 1+gamma m. The cone inequality follows from nonnegative support. Statement unchanged by this proof repair."
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "For $x\\ge0$, $\\operatorname{Im}(z/(x-z))=\\eta x/|x-z|^2\\ge0$. Integrating this identity against the probability measure from step 1.1 gives $\\operatorname{Im}(z m_\\gamma(z))\\ge0$, so $\\operatorname{Im}(1-\\gamma-z-\\gamma z m_\\gamma(z))=-\\eta-\\gamma\\operatorname{Im}(z m_\\gamma(z))\\le-\\eta$.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
