        "notes": "Checked mass integral, including cancellation at gamma=1, Borel density construction under countable choice, connected slit-plane square-root branch and holomorphic parameter integral, both atom cases and quadratic algebra. Confirmed refuter nonfatal omission: strict positivity of Im m now explicitly licenses division by 1+gamma m. The cone inequality follows from nonnegative support. Statement unchanged by this proof repair."
      }
    },
    "lem-stieltjes-transform-inversion-and-uniqueness-on-the-real-line": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-stieltjes-transform-of-a-real-probability-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mu$ be a Borel probability measure on $\\mathbb R$. For $z=u+i\\eta$ with $u\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\eta>0$, its Stieltjes transform, with the $(x-z)^{-1}$ convention, is\n$$m_\\mu(z):=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\frac{1}{x-z}\\,d\\mu(x).$$\nThe integral is finite because $|x-z|^{-1}\\le\\eta^{-1}$ for every real $x$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. All functions below have domain $X$. Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-dynkin-pi-lambda",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal P$ be a pi-system on $X$. Then\n$\\lambda_X(\\mathcal P)=\\sigma_X(\\mathcal P)$. Consequently, if $\\mathcal D$ is\nany lambda-system on $X$ with $\\mathcal P\\subseteq\\mathcal D$, then\n$\\sigma_X(\\mathcal P)\\subseteq\\mathcal D$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets (Finite, countably infinite, countable, uncountable) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix $n \\ge 1$ and let $\\lambda_n^*$ be the Lebesgue outer set function on $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n(Lebesgue outer measure on $\\mathbb{R}^n$). A set $E \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n**Lebesgue measurable** when\n\n$$\\lambda_n^*(A) \\;=\\; \\lambda_n^*(A \\cap E) \\;+\\; \\lambda_n^*(A \\setminus E) \\qquad \\text{for every } A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n .$$\n\nThis formula makes sense before any outer-measure theorem is invoked. Under\ncountable choice, Assuming countable choice, Lebesgue outer measure is an outer measure that restricts to elementary volume\nmakes $\\lambda_n^*$ an outer measure, and the displayed condition is then exactly\nCarathéodory measurability in the sense of Carathéodory measurable sets.\n\nThe family of Lebesgue measurable sets is written $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$,\nand **Lebesgue measure** is the restriction\n\n$$\\lambda_n \\;:=\\; \\lambda_n^*\\!\\restriction_{\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)} .$$\n\nOn the real line the subscript is dropped and $\\lambda := \\lambda_1$.\n\nThe quantifier over every test set $A$ is part of the condition, and no\nhypothesis on $E$ is imposed before it is tested. That\n$\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$ is a sigma-algebra and that $\\lambda_n$ is a complete\nmeasure on it are not part of this definition: they are proved, under the Axiom\nof Countable Choice, in Assuming countable choice, $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$ is a sigma-algebra containing every elementary set and $\\lambda_n$ is a complete measure extending elementary volume, which is\nrecorded in this item's `justified_by` because it is a statement about the\nobjects introduced here. Until that theorem the symbols $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$\nand $\\lambda_n$ name a family of sets and a restricted set function, nothing more.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-lebesgue-measure-is-a-complete-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\ge 1$ and **assume the Axiom of Countable Choice**\n(The Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$)). Then:\n\n1. $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$ is a sigma-algebra on $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and\n   $\\lambda_n$ is a measure on it (Measures on sigma-algebras);\n2. the measure space $(\\mathbb{R}^n, \\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n), \\lambda_n)$ is\n   complete (Complete measure spaces), and every $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$\n   with $\\lambda_n^*(S) = 0$ is Lebesgue measurable with $\\lambda_n(S) = 0$;\n3. every elementary set is Lebesgue measurable and\n   $\\lambda_n(A) = \\mu_0(A)$ for every $A \\in \\mathcal{E}_n$; in particular\n   $\\lambda_n(B) = \\operatorname{vol}(B)$ for every half-open box $B$,\n   $\\lambda_n(\\varnothing) = 0$ and $\\lambda_n(\\mathbb{R}^n) = +\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-borel-sets-are-lebesgue-measurable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\ge 1$ and **assume the Axiom of Countable Choice**\n(The Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$)). Then\n\n$$\\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^n) \\;\\subseteq\\; \\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n) :$$\n\nevery Borel subset of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ (The Borel sigma-algebra of a topological space) is Lebesgue\nmeasurable (Lebesgue measurable sets, the family $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$, and the restricted set function $\\lambda_n$). In\nparticular every open set, every closed set and every countable intersection of\nopen sets is Lebesgue measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "The kernel $K(x,u)=\\eta/((x-u)^2+\\eta^2)$ is continuous on $\\mathbb R^2$ and hence Borel measurable; rational open rectangles form a countable basis, so $\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^2)=\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R)\\otimes\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R)\\subseteq\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R)\\otimes\\mathcal L(\\mathbb R)$. Restricting the second coordinate to the Lebesgue-measurable interval $[a,b]$ preserves product measurability. Its integral in $u$ is $$\\int_a^b\\frac{\\eta}{(x-u)^2+\\eta^2}\\,du=\\arctan\\frac{b-x}{\\eta}-\\arctan\\frac{a-x}{\\eta}\\le\\pi.$$ By [F6], Lebesgue measure restricted to $[a,b]$ is finite, and the probability measure $\\mu$ is finite. Tonelli [F5] shows that the product integrand has integral at most $\\pi\\mu(\\mathbb R)=\\pi$, so it belongs to $L^1$ on the product space. Fubini [F2] therefore gives $$\\frac1\\pi\\int_a^b\\operatorname{Im}m_\\mu(u+i\\eta)\\,du=\\int K_{\\eta,a,b}(x)\\,d\\mu(x),\\quad K_{\\eta,a,b}(x)=\\frac1\\pi\\left(\\arctan\\frac{b-x}{\\eta}-\\arctan\\frac{a-x}{\\eta}\\right).$$ The kernel lies in $[0,1]$ and tends pointwise to $1$ on $(a,b)$, $1/2$ at either endpoint, and $0$ elsewhere. [F3] gives the interval formula in the Statement, including endpoint atoms.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "given",
            "algebra",
            "A1",
            "F6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "For any $x\\in\\mathbb R$, $$\\eta\\operatorname{Im}m_\\mu(x+i\\eta)=\\int\\frac{\\eta^2}{(t-x)^2+\\eta^2}\\,d\\mu(t).$$ The integrand is bounded by $1$ and tends to $\\mathbf1_{\\{x\\}}(t)$ as $\\eta\\downarrow0$. Dominated convergence [F3] proves the atom formula.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "If $m_\\mu=m_\\nu$, step 1.2 gives equality of their point masses at every $x$. Step 1.1 then gives $\\mu((a,b))=\\nu((a,b))$ for every $a<b$.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2-2",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "The family $\\mathcal P=\\{\\varnothing,\\mathbb R\\}\\cup\\{(a,b):a<b\\}$ is a pi-system: the intersection of two open intervals is another open interval or empty. It generates the Borel sigma-algebra because open intervals form a basis. The class of Borel sets on which $\\mu$ and $\\nu$ agree is a lambda-system: it contains $\\mathbb R$ because both measures are probabilities, is closed under complements by finite total mass, and is closed under disjoint countable unions by countable additivity. Step 2.1 and [F4] therefore give equality on every Borel set, so $\\mu=\\nu$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
