    "thm-marchenko-pastur-law-for-sample-covariance": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-sample-covariance-empirical-spectral-distribution",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For positive integers $n$ and $p$, let $X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p}$ and define its (uncentered, unit-variance-normalized) sample covariance matrix by\n$$S(X):=\\frac1nX^{\\mathsf T}X\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times p}.$$\nIt is real symmetric and non-negative. If its eigenvalues, counted with multiplicity, are $\\lambda_1(S(X)),\\ldots,\\lambda_p(S(X))$, its empirical spectral distribution (ESD), normalized by the matrix dimension $p$, is\n$$\\mu_{S(X)}:=\\frac1p\\sum_{j=1}^p\\delta_{\\lambda_j(S(X))}.$$\nThe largest eigenvalue is $\\lambda_{\\max}(S(X)):=\\max_j\\lambda_j(S(X))$. When $S(X)$ has at least one positive eigenvalue, its smallest positive eigenvalue is $\\lambda_{\\min}^{+}(S(X)):=\\min\\{\\lambda_j(S(X)):\\lambda_j(S(X))>0\\}$. For a sequence $(X_n)_{n\\ge1}$ with $X_n\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p_n}$ and positive integer $p_n$ for every $n\\ge1$, write $S_n=S(X_n)$ and $\\mu_n=\\mu_{S_n}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-sample-covariance-empirical-law-is-a-probability-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For positive integers $n,p$ and a real matrix $X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p}$, set $S(X)=X^{\\mathsf T}X/n$ and $\\mu_{S(X)}=p^{-1}\\sum_{j=1}^p\\delta_{\\lambda_j(S(X))}$, where the eigenvalues are counted with multiplicity. Then $\\mu_{S(X)}$ is a Borel probability measure on $\\mathbb R$, supported on $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-stieltjes-transform-of-a-real-probability-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mu$ be a Borel probability measure on $\\mathbb R$. For $z=u+i\\eta$ with $u\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\eta>0$, its Stieltjes transform, with the $(x-z)^{-1}$ convention, is\n$$m_\\mu(z):=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\frac{1}{x-z}\\,d\\mu(x).$$\nThe integral is finite because $|x-z|^{-1}\\le\\eta^{-1}$ for every real $x$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-fourth-moment-quadratic-form-resolvent-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\xi=(\\xi_1,\\ldots,\\xi_d)^{\\mathsf T}$ have independent identically distributed real coordinates with $\\mathbb E\\xi_i=0$, $\\mathbb E\\xi_i^2=1$, and $\\mu_4:=\\mathbb E\\xi_i^4<\\infty$. Let $A$ be an independent random complex symmetric $d\\times d$ matrix with $\\mathbb E\\|A\\|_F^2<\\infty$, where $\\|A\\|_F^2:=\\sum_{i,j}|A_{ij}|^2$. Then\n$$\\mathbb E\\left|\\xi^{\\mathsf T}A\\xi-\\operatorname{tr}A\\right|^2\\le C_{\\mu_4}\\,\\mathbb E\\|A\\|_F^2,\\qquad C_{\\mu_4}:=\\max\\{\\mu_4-1,2\\}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-rank-one-resolvent-update-and-trace-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $B$ be a real symmetric non-negative $d\\times d$ matrix, let $x\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and let $z=u+i\\eta$ with $u\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\eta>0$. Put $R=(B-zI)^{-1}$ and $R_x=(B+xx^{\\mathsf T}-zI)^{-1}$. Then $1+x^{\\mathsf T}Rx\\ne0$,\n$$R_x=R-\\frac{Rxx^{\\mathsf T}R}{1+x^{\\mathsf T}Rx},$$\nand\n$$\\left|\\operatorname{tr}R_x-\\operatorname{tr}R\\right|\\le\\frac1\\eta.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-adapted-resolvent-trace-concentration",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice, and let all random vectors below be defined on one common probability space. For each positive integer $n$, let $d_n$ be a positive integer, let $Y_{n,1},\\ldots,Y_{n,n}$ be independent random vectors in $\\mathbb R^{d_n}$, let $B_n=\\sum_{j=1}^{n}Y_{n,j}Y_{n,j}^{\\mathsf T}$, and let $z=u+i\\eta$ with fixed $u\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\eta>0$. Define\n$$M_n(z):=\\frac1{d_n}\\operatorname{tr}(B_n-zI)^{-1}.$$\nIf $d_n/n\\to c$ for some $c\\in(0,\\infty)$, then $M_n(z)-\\mathbb E[M_n(z)]\\to0$ almost surely. More precisely, for every $t>0$,\n$$\\Pr\\left\\{|M_n(z)-\\mathbb E M_n(z)|>t\\right\\} \\le4\\exp\\left(-\\frac{t^2d_n^2\\eta^2}{4n}\\right).$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-marchenko-pastur-formula-defines-a-finite-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice.\n\nFor every $\\gamma>0$, the set function $\\mu_\\gamma$ in the definition of the Marchenko–Pastur measure is a Borel probability measure supported on $[0,\\infty)$. For every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ with $\\eta:=\\operatorname{Im}z>0$, its Stieltjes transform $m_\\gamma(z)$ satisfies\n$$1+z m_\\gamma(z)=\\frac{m_\\gamma(z)}{1+\\gamma m_\\gamma(z)},\\qquad \\operatorname{Im}\\bigl(1-\\gamma-z-\\gamma z m_\\gamma(z)\\bigr)\\le-\\eta.$$",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-stieltjes-transform-inversion-and-uniqueness-on-the-real-line",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice.\n\nLet $\\mu$ be a Borel probability measure on $\\mathbb R$, with Stieltjes transform $m_\\mu(z)=\\int (x-z)^{-1}\\,d\\mu(x)$ for $\\operatorname{Im}z>0$. For every $a<b$ and $x\\in\\mathbb R$,\n$$\\mu((a,b))+\\tfrac12\\mu(\\{a\\})+\\tfrac12\\mu(\\{b\\})=\\lim_{\\eta\\downarrow0}\\frac1\\pi\\int_a^b\\operatorname{Im}m_\\mu(u+i\\eta)\\,du,\\qquad \\mu(\\{x\\})=\\lim_{\\eta\\downarrow0}\\eta\\,\\operatorname{Im}m_\\mu(x+i\\eta).$$\nIf two Borel probability measures on $\\mathbb R$ have the same Stieltjes transform on the upper half-plane, then they are equal.",
          "uses": [
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "cor-tightness-extracts-a-weakly-convergent-subsequence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. A tight sequence of Borel probability laws on a Polish S has a subsequence converging weakly to a Borel probability on that same S.",
          "uses": [
            "6.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-chebyshev-inequality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every finite real random variable $X$ and every $t>0$,\n$$\\mathbb P(|X-\\mathbb E[X]|\\ge t)\\le\\frac{\\operatorname{Var}(X)}{t^2}.$$\nEquivalently, if $\\sigma_X>0$ and $c>0$, then $\\mathbb P(|X-\\mathbb E[X]|\\ge c\\sigma_X)\\le1/c^2$. If $\\sigma_X=0$, the first form remains valid for every $t>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "cor-first-borel-cantelli-lemma-for-events",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(A_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ be events in a probability space. If\n$$\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\mathbb P(A_n)<+\\infty,$$\nthen\n$$\\mathbb P(A_n\\ \\mathrm{i.o.})=0.$$\n\nNo independence hypothesis is needed.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "def-weak-convergence-of-borel-probability-measures",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For Borel probability measures $\\mu_n,\\mu$ on a metric space S, write $\\mu_n\\Rightarrow\\mu$ if $\\int f\\,d\\mu_n\\to\\int f\\,d\\mu$ for every bounded continuous real function f on S. Continuity is Continuity of a map between metric spaces, at a point and globally, in the $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ form. Such f is Borel measurable (inverse images of open sets are open) and $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu\\le\\|f\\|_\\infty\\mu(S)<\\infty$, so the integrals are finite in Integrable real and complex functions, and their integrals. No completeness or coupling is required.",
          "uses": [
            "6.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be random elements with the same measurable target $(E,\\mathcal E)$. They are **identically distributed** if $\\mathbb P(X_i\\in B)=\\mathbb P(X_j\\in B)$ for all $i,j\\in I$ and $B\\in\\mathcal E$, that is, their laws in Law or distribution of a random element agree. They are **independent and identically distributed (IID)** if, in addition, the whole family is independent in Independent random elements. Independence means mutual independence, not merely pairwise independence. No moment assumption is part of either definition. The empty family satisfies these universal conditions vacuously.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "def-almost-sure-convergence-of-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_n)$ and $X$ be real random variables on one probability space.  Write\n$X_n\\to X$ **almost surely** when\n$$\\mathbb P\\bigl(\\{\\omega:X_n(\\omega)\\to X(\\omega)\\text{ in }\\mathbb R\\}\\bigr)=1.$$\nThe convergence in the event is the real convergence of Limits and Cauchy sequences of reals.\nThe set is measurable by The almost-sure convergence event is measurable,\nso its probability is defined.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "thm-choice-implies-dependent-implies-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In ZF,\n\n$$\\mathrm{AC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{DC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega,\\mathrm{fin}}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega,2},\\qquad \\mathrm{AC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_2.$$\n\nDC here includes a prescribed initial point.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Fix $z=u+i\\eta$ with $\\eta>0$. Write the transpose of row $i$ of $X_n$ as $y_i\\in\\mathbb R^{p_n}$ and set $Q=(S_n-zI)^{-1}$, $m_n=p_n^{-1}\\operatorname{tr}Q=m_{\\mu_n}(z)$ by [F1] and [F2]. For each $i$, let $S_i=S_n-y_iy_i^{\\mathsf T}/n$, $Q_i=(S_i-zI)^{-1}$ and $\\beta_i=n^{-1}y_i^{\\mathsf T}Q_i y_i$. The matrices $S_n,S_i$ are nonnegative symmetric, so their resolvents exist; diagonalizing $S_i$ expresses $\\beta_i$ as a sum of nonnegative weights times $(\\lambda-z)^{-1}$ with $\\lambda\\ge0$. Thus $\\operatorname{Im}\\beta_i\\ge0$ and $\\operatorname{Im}(z\\beta_i)\\ge0$, since $\\operatorname{Im}[z/(\\lambda-z)]=\\eta\\lambda/|\\lambda-z|^2\\ge0$. Consequently $\\operatorname{Im}[z(1+\\beta_i)]\\ge\\eta$, so $|1+\\beta_i|\\ge\\eta/|z|>0$. Applying [F4] with $x=y_i/\\sqrt n$ gives $Q=Q_i-Q_i y_i y_i^{\\mathsf T}Q_i/[n(1+\\beta_i)]$, so $n^{-1}y_i^{\\mathsf T}Qy_i=\\beta_i/(1+\\beta_i)$. Since $S_nQ=I+zQ$, taking normalized traces yields $$1+z m_n=\\frac1{p_n}\\sum_{i=1}^n\\frac{\\beta_i}{1+\\beta_i}.$$",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-2",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Put $\\gamma_n=p_n/n$, $v_n=\\gamma_n m_n=n^{-1}\\operatorname{tr}Q$, and $\\mu_4=\\mathbb E\\xi^4$ for the common entry law. Both $\\beta_i$ and $v_n$ belong to the cone $\\mathcal C_z=\\{w:\\operatorname{Im}w\\ge0,\\ \\operatorname{Im}(zw)\\ge0\\}$: for $v_n$ use the same eigenvalue calculation on $Q$. For every $w\\in\\mathcal C_z$, $\\operatorname{Im}[z(1+w)]\\ge\\eta$, so $|1+w|^{-1}\\le|z|/\\eta$. Conditional on $Q_i$, row $y_i$ is independent of it by [F12]. Applying [F3] conditionally and using $\\|Q_i\\|_F^2\\le p_n/\\eta^2$ gives $$\\mathbb E\\left|\\beta_i-\\frac1n\\operatorname{tr}Q_i\\right|\\le\\frac{\\sqrt{C p_n}}{n\\eta},\\qquad C=\\max\\{\\mu_4-1,2\\}.$$ By [F4], $|\\operatorname{tr}Q_i-\\operatorname{tr}Q|\\le1/\\eta$, hence $\\mathbb E|\\beta_i-v_n|\\le(\\sqrt{Cp_n}+1)/(n\\eta)$. For $f(w)=w/(1+w)$ and $w,v\\in\\mathcal C_z$, $|f(w)-f(v)|=|w-v|/(|1+w||1+v|)\\le K_z|w-v|$, where $K_z=(|z|/\\eta)^2$. Therefore [step 1.1] implies $$\\mathbb E\\left|1+zm_n-\\frac{m_n}{1+\\gamma_n m_n}\\right|\\le K_z\\frac{\\sqrt{Cp_n}+1}{p_n\\eta}\\longrightarrow0.$$",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F12",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-3",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Apply [F5] to the independent vectors $y_i/\\sqrt n$, whose rank-one sum is $S_n$. For the fixed $z$, $m_n-a_n\\to0$ almost surely, where $a_n=\\mathbb E m_n$. Also $\\mathbb E|m_n-a_n|\\to0$: for every $\\varepsilon>0$, $\\mathbb E|m_n-a_n|\\le\\varepsilon+(2/\\eta)\\Pr(|m_n-a_n|>\\varepsilon)$, and the tail bound in [F5] makes the second term tend to zero. Define $g_n(w)=1+zw-w/(1+\\gamma_nw)$. The cone $\\mathcal C_z$ is closed under nonnegative scaling and expectations, so it contains $m_n,a_n,\\gamma_nm_n$ and $\\gamma_na_n$. For $w,v\\in\\mathcal C_z$, the denominator estimate of step 2.1 applied to $\\gamma_nw,\\gamma_nv$ gives $|g_n(w)-g_n(v)|\\le(|z|+K_z)|w-v|$. Taking expectations in the estimate of step 2.1 gives $g_n(a_n)\\to0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F13",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-4",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "The sequence $(a_n)$ is bounded by $1/\\eta$. Moreover $\\operatorname{Im}(z m_n)=p_n^{-1}\\sum_j\\eta\\lambda_j(S_n)/|\\lambda_j(S_n)-z|^2\\ge0$, so $\\operatorname{Im}(1-\\gamma_n-z-\\gamma_n z a_n)\\le-\\eta$. The same closed-cone bound gives $|1+\\gamma_na_n|\\ge\\eta/|z|$. Every convergent subsequence of $a_n$ therefore has a limit $a$, with $|1+\\gamma a|\\ge\\eta/|z|$, satisfying $1+za=a/(1+\\gamma a)$ and $\\operatorname{Im}(1-\\gamma-z-\\gamma za)\\le-\\eta$. The two roots of the associated quadratic have corresponding values $D_j=1-\\gamma-z-\\gamma z a_j$ whose sum is $1-\\gamma-z$, with imaginary part $-\\eta$. Thus at most one root can have $\\operatorname{Im}D_j\\le-\\eta$. By [A2] and [F6], that root is $m_\\gamma(z)$. All subsequential limits are consequently $m_\\gamma(z)$, so $a_n\\to m_\\gamma(z)$ and step 3.1 gives $m_n(z)\\to m_\\gamma(z)$ almost surely.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F6",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra",
            "A2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-5",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "Perform steps 1.1–4.1 for each $z$ in the countable dense set $\\{u+i\\eta:u,\\eta\\in\\mathbb Q,\\eta>0\\}$ and intersect their probability-one events. Also let $T_n=(np_n)^{-1}\\sum_{i,j}X_{n,ij}^2$. Its mean is one and its variance is $(\\mu_4-1)/(np_n)$. Since $p_n/n\\to\\gamma>0$, [F9] gives a summable bound $\\Pr(|T_n-1|>\\varepsilon)\\le(\\mu_4-1)/(\\varepsilon^2np_n)$ for every $\\varepsilon>0$; [F10] over positive rational $\\varepsilon$ yields $T_n\\to1$ almost surely.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "F12",
            "F13",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra",
            "common probability space"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-6",
          "step": "6.1",
          "claim": "On this probability-one event, $\\int x\\,d\\mu_n(x)=T_n$ and $\\mu_n$ is supported on $[0,\\infty)$ by [F1]. For every $R>0$, $\\mu_n((R,\\infty))\\le T_n/R$, so the sequence is tight. By [A1] and [F8], every subsequence has a further weakly convergent subsequence with limit $\\nu$. Along it, [F11] applied to $x\\mapsto(x-z)^{-1}$ shows $m_\\nu(z)=m_\\gamma(z)$ on the dense set from step 5.1. The transforms are continuous because $|m_\\nu(z)-m_\\nu(w)|\\le|z-w|/(\\operatorname{Im}z\\operatorname{Im}w)$ for $z,w\\in\\mathbb C^+$; equality therefore extends to all $z\\in\\mathbb C^+$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F8",
            "F11",
            "step 5.1",
            "A1",
            "algebra",
            "common probability space"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-7",
          "step": "7.1",
          "claim": "By [A2] and [F7], every weak subsequential limit $\\nu$ in step 6.1 equals $\\mu_\\gamma$. If $\\mu_n$ did not converge weakly to $\\mu_\\gamma$, some bounded continuous test function and a subsequence would have integrals separated from its $\\mu_\\gamma$ integral by a fixed positive amount; tightness would give a further weakly convergent subsequence, contradicting its limit $\\mu_\\gamma$. Therefore $\\mu_n\\Rightarrow\\mu_\\gamma$ almost surely.",
          "inputs": [
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F11",
            "step 6.1",
            "algebra",
            "common probability space",
            "A2"
          ]
        }
      ],
