    "ex-marchenko-pastur-law-and-edges-at-aspect-four": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-marchenko-pastur-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice.\n\nFor $\\gamma>0$, set\n$$a_\\gamma=(1-\\sqrt\\gamma)^2,\\qquad b_\\gamma=(1+\\sqrt\\gamma)^2,\\qquad r_+:=\\max\\{r,0\\}\\quad(r\\in\\mathbb R).$$\nDefine the Marchenko–Pastur set function on Borel $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ by\n$$\\mu_\\gamma(A):=\\int_{A\\cap(a_\\gamma,b_\\gamma)}\\frac{\\sqrt{(b_\\gamma-x)(x-a_\\gamma)}}{2\\pi\\gamma x}\\,d\\lambda(x)+\\left(1-\\frac1\\gamma\\right)_+\\delta_0(A),$$\nwhere $\\lambda$ is Lebesgue measure on $\\mathbb R$. The formula defines a finite Borel measure; its total mass is established by the Marchenko–Pastur law below.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-marchenko-pastur-law-for-sample-covariance",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice, and let all the matrices $X_n$ be defined on one common probability space. For each positive integer $n$, let $p_n$ be a positive integer with $p_n/n\\to\\gamma\\in(0,\\infty)$, and let $X_n\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p_n}$ have mutually independent entries. Assume that all entries of all the matrices $X_n$ have one common law with mean zero, variance one, and finite fourth moment. Set $S_n=X_n^{\\mathsf T}X_n/n$ and let $\\mu_n$ be its empirical spectral distribution normalized by $p_n$. Then $\\mu_n$ converges weakly almost surely to the probability measure with density\n$$\\rho_\\gamma(x)=\\begin{cases}\\dfrac{\\sqrt{(b_\\gamma-x)(x-a_\\gamma)}}{2\\pi\\gamma x},&a_\\gamma<x<b_\\gamma,\\\\0,&x\\notin(a_\\gamma,b_\\gamma),\\end{cases}\\qquad a_\\gamma=(1-\\sqrt\\gamma)^2,\\quad b_\\gamma=(1+\\sqrt\\gamma)^2,$$\nand an atom of mass $(1-1/\\gamma)_+$ at zero.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "thm-largest-eigenvalue-sample-covariance-edge",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\xi_{ij})_{i,j\\ge1}$ be one infinite iid array of real random variables with mean zero, variance one and finite fourth moment. For each positive integer $n$, let $p_n$ be a positive integer and let $X_n=(\\xi_{ij})_{1\\le i\\le n,1\\le j\\le p_n}$ and suppose $p_n/n\\to\\gamma\\in(0,\\infty)$. For $S_n=X_n^{\\mathsf T}X_n/n$, the largest eigenvalue satisfies\n$$\\lambda_{\\max}(S_n)\\longrightarrow(1+\\sqrt\\gamma)^2\\quad\\text{almost surely}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "thm-smallest-positive-eigenvalue-sample-covariance-edge",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\xi_{ij})_{i,j\\ge1}$ be one infinite iid array of real random variables with mean zero, variance one and finite fourth moment. For each positive integer $n$, let $p_n$ be a positive integer and let $X_n=(\\xi_{ij})_{1\\le i\\le n,1\\le j\\le p_n}$ and suppose $p_n/n\\to\\gamma\\in(0,\\infty)$. Set $S_n=X_n^{\\mathsf T}X_n/n$ and let $\\lambda_{\\min}^{+}(S_n)$ denote its smallest positive eigenvalue whenever one exists. If $\\gamma<1$, then almost surely $X_n$ has full column rank for all sufficiently large $n$, so $S_n$ has no zero eigenvalues eventually and $\\lambda_{\\min}(S_n)\\to(1-\\sqrt\\gamma)^2$. If $\\gamma>1$, then almost surely $X_n$ has full row rank for all sufficiently large $n$, so $S_n$ has exactly $p_n-n$ zero eigenvalues and $\\lambda_{\\min}^{+}(S_n)\\to(\\sqrt\\gamma-1)^2$. If $\\gamma=1$, then almost surely $\\lambda_{\\min}^{+}(S_n)\\to0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "thm-choice-implies-dependent-implies-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In ZF,\n\n$$\\mathrm{AC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{DC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega,\\mathrm{fin}}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega,2},\\qquad \\mathrm{AC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_2.$$\n\nDC here includes a prescribed initial point.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Since $\\sqrt\\gamma=2$, the endpoints are $a=(1-2)^2=1$ and $b=(1+2)^2=9$, while the zero atom is $(1-1/4)_+=3/4$. Substituting in [F1] gives the displayed density on $(1,9)$ and zero outside, plus the atom $3/4$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra",
            "A2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-2",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The nested array and AC satisfy [L1]–[L3]. The MP weak law [L1] gives the limiting density and atom computed in step 1.1. Independently, [L2] gives largest edge $9$; [L3] gives smallest positive edge $1$ and eventual full row rank, hence exactly $p_n-n$ zero eigenvalues. Intersecting these probability-one events proves the assertions.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "L3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
