    "lem-uniform-row-partial-sums-of-nested-finite-variance-array": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be random elements with the same measurable target $(E,\\mathcal E)$. They are **identically distributed** if $\\mathbb P(X_i\\in B)=\\mathbb P(X_j\\in B)$ for all $i,j\\in I$ and $B\\in\\mathcal E$, that is, their laws in Law or distribution of a random element agree. They are **independent and identically distributed (IID)** if, in addition, the whole family is independent in Independent random elements. Independence means mutual independence, not merely pairwise independence. No moment assumption is part of either definition. The empty family satisfies these universal conditions vacuously.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "cor-first-borel-cantelli-lemma-for-events",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(A_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ be events in a probability space. If\n$$\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\mathbb P(A_n)<+\\infty,$$\nthen\n$$\\mathbb P(A_n\\ \\mathrm{i.o.})=0.$$\n\nNo independence hypothesis is needed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "If $C\\le0$ the claim is vacuous. Suppose $C>0$, set $v=EZ_{11}^2$ and fix $\\varepsilon>0$. For a dyadic integer $m=2^\\ell$ put $N=2m$, $I=\\lceil2Cm\\rceil$, $x=\\varepsilon m$, $r=4$ and $y=x/r$. For one row write $Z_j=Z_{ij}$ and $W_j=Z_j\\mathbf1_{\\{Z_j\\le y\\}}$. These variables are independent with $EW_j=-E[Z_j\\mathbf1_{\\{Z_j>y\\}}]\\le0$ and $EW_j^2\\le v$. For $t>0$ and $w\\le y$, $e^{tw}\\le1+tw+(e^{ty}-1-ty)w^2/y^2$: the function $(e^u-1-u)/u^2=\\int_0^1(1-s)e^{su}\\,ds$, extended continuously at zero, is increasing in $u$. Consequently $Ee^{tW_j}\\le\\exp\\{v(e^{ty}-1-ty)/y^2\\}$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-2",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Set $\\psi=v(e^{ty}-1-ty)/y^2\\ge0$. Independence shows that $M_k=\\exp(t\\sum_{j\\le k}W_j-k\\psi)$ has conditional expected next value at most $M_k$. If $\\tau$ is the first $k\\le N$ with $\\sum_{j\\le k}W_j\\ge x$, stopping this finite sequence at $\\tau$ or $N$ preserves the expectation bound $1$: condition each increment and observe that the indicator of not having stopped is determined by the preceding variables. On a crossing its stopped value is at least $e^{tx-N\\psi}$. Nonnegativity therefore gives $P(\\max_{k\\le N}\\sum_{j\\le k}W_j\\ge x)\\le e^{-tx+N\\psi}$. This proves the needed maximal bound directly.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-3",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "If $v=0$, every $Z_j=0$ almost surely and the conclusion follows. Otherwise choose $t>0$ by $e^{ty}=1+xy/(Nv)$. Then $N\\psi\\le x/y=r$, so the preceding probability is at most $e^r(Nv/(xy))^r\\le K m^{-r}$, with $K$ depending only on $v,\\varepsilon$. On the event that all $Z_j\\le y$, the original and truncated partial sums agree; the complement has probability at most $NP(Z_{11}>y)$. Thus the positive original maximal tail is at most $NP(Z_{11}>y)+Km^{-r}$. Apply the same argument to $-Z_j$ for the negative tail and use a union bound over the $I$ rows. The result is $$P\\left(\\max_{i\\le I,k\\le N}\\left|\\sum_{j\\le k}Z_{ij}\\right|\\ge\\varepsilon m\\right)\\le K_Cm^2P(|Z_{11}|>\\varepsilon m/4)+K'_Cm^{1-r}.$$",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-4",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "The right-hand side is summable over dyadic $m$: for every $a>0$, $\\sum_\\ell2^{2\\ell}P(|Z_{11}|>a2^\\ell)\\le K_aE|Z_{11}|^2<\\infty$, by summing the finite geometric progression $\\sum_{\\ell:\\,a2^\\ell<|Z_{11}|}2^{2\\ell}$ under expectation. Also $\\sum_\\ell2^{(1-r)\\ell}<\\infty$. First Borel–Cantelli gives the displayed maximal estimate eventually on each dyadic block. Whenever $m\\le n\\le2m$, every $i\\le Cn$ and $k\\le n$ occurs in the corresponding block; division by $n\\ge m$ bounds the desired ratio by $\\varepsilon$ eventually. Intersecting over positive rational $\\varepsilon$ proves convergence to zero. Exchanging the two coordinates preserves the iid law and gives the column version.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The uniform partial-sum lemma gives the empty-maximum convention before its dyadic maximal-tail argument. Proof 1.1 handles a nonpositive row multiplier by the stated empty maximum equal to zero, and the same convention handles any initial zero row count.",
