    "lem-nested-iid-fourth-moment-truncation-and-diagonal-control": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "cor-first-borel-cantelli-lemma-for-events",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(A_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ be events in a probability space. If\n$$\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\mathbb P(A_n)<+\\infty,$$\nthen\n$$\\mathbb P(A_n\\ \\mathrm{i.o.})=0.$$\n\nNo independence hypothesis is needed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-uniform-row-partial-sums-of-nested-finite-variance-array",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(Z_{ij})_{i,j\\ge1}$ be one infinite iid real array with $EZ_{11}=0$ and $EZ_{11}^2<\\infty$. For every fixed $C<\\infty$,\n$$\\frac1n\\max_{1\\le i\\le\\lfloor Cn\\rfloor}\\max_{1\\le k\\le n}\\left|\\sum_{j=1}^{k}Z_{ij}\\right|\\longrightarrow0\\quad\\text{almost surely}.$$\nThe maximum over an empty index set is zero. The same conclusion holds with row and column coordinates exchanged.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-kolmogorov-iid-l1-strong-law",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For IID real $(X_n)_{n\\ge1}$ with $\\mathbb E|X_1|<\\infty$, $S_n/n\\to\\mu=\\mathbb EX_1$ almost surely.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. All functions below have domain $X$. Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Fix a finite $D$ with $R_n,C_n\\le Dn$ and $N_n\\ge n/D$ eventually. For every $\\varepsilon>0$, the union bound on a square of side $D2^{\\ell+1}$ gives a constant times $2^{2\\ell}P(|\\xi_{11}|>\\varepsilon2^{\\ell/2})$ as a bound for the probability of any such large entry in that square. Its series over $\\ell$ is finite: for every $t\\ge0$ the geometric sum $\\sum_{\\ell:\\,\\varepsilon2^{\\ell/2}<t}2^{2\\ell}$ is at most a constant times $\\varepsilon^{-4}t^4$, so expectation proves the assertion. F1 on dyadic blocks, followed by intersection over rational $\\varepsilon>0$, proves $\\max_{i\\le R_n,j\\le C_n}|\\xi_{ij}|/\\sqrt{N_n}\\to0$ almost surely.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-2",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The dyadic probability series in step 1.1 converges separately for every threshold $\\varepsilon=1/a$. Choose deterministic integers $L_a$ increasing so that its tail from $L_a$ onward is at most $2^{-a}$; impose also $L_a\\ge a$, and every prescribed slow-rate constraint by increasing $L_a$ further. On the blocks $L_a\\le\\ell<L_{a+1}$ choose the truncation threshold as a suitable fixed multiple of $2^{\\ell/2}/a$, large enough to cover the comparable dimensions and the adjacent dyadic interval. The sum of the resulting block probabilities is at most $\\sum_a2^{-a}$. First Borel–Cantelli proves eventual equality to these truncations. A stepwise deterministic enlargement gives $\\delta_n\\to0$ as slowly as required, with $\\delta_n\\ge1/\\log(n+2)$; enlarging a threshold preserves eventual equality. One may absorb every fixed dimensional comparison constant into $\\delta_n$. Since $\\delta_n\\sqrt{N_n}\\to\\infty$, enlarge finitely many initial values further so that $\\delta_n\\sqrt{N_n}\\ge\\max(1,E|\\xi_{11}|)$ for every $n$; this preserves all asymptotic and eventual-equality conclusions.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-3",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Since $E\\xi=0$, $|\\mu_n|\\le E[|\\xi|\\mathbf1_{|\\xi|>\\delta_n\\sqrt{N_n}}]\\le E|\\xi|^4/(\\delta_n^3N_n^{3/2})$. Therefore $\\sqrt{R_nC_n/N_n}|\\mu_n|\\le K\\delta_n^{-3}/N_n\\to0$, by the selected slow rate. Dominated convergence gives $E|x_{11}^{(n)}|^2=E[\\xi^2\\mathbf1_{|\\xi|\\le\\delta_n\\sqrt{N_n}}]-\\mu_n^2\\to1$, and this variance is at most $1$. Moreover $|\\mu_n|\\le E|\\xi|\\le\\delta_n\\sqrt{N_n}$ for every $n$, so $|x_{ij}^{(n)}|\\le L_n:=2\\delta_n\\sqrt{N_n}$ for every $n$. The third absolute moments are bounded by $4(E|\\xi|^3+|\\mu_n|^3)\\le4(E|\\xi|^3+(E|\\xi|)^3)$, uniformly in $n$. Thus for $q\\ge3$, $E|x|^q\\le L_n^{q-3}E|x|^3\\le KL_n^{q-3}$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-4",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "Apply F2 to the infinite centered finite-variance array $Z_{ij}=\\xi_{ij}^2-1$. Using a fixed enlargement of the row bound and of the maximal partial-sum index covers every $R_n,C_n\\le Dn$; division by $N_n\\ge n/D$ proves the stated uniform row and column second-moment laws for the original entries. On the probability-one event of eventual truncation equality, $x_{ij}^{(n)}=\\xi_{ij}-\\mu_n$ throughout the rectangle. The difference of a normalized row squared sum from the original one is bounded by $2|\\mu_n|\\sqrt{(C_n/N_n)(N_n^{-1}\\sum_j\\xi_{ij}^2)}+(C_n/N_n)\\mu_n^2$, by Cauchy–Schwarz; its maximum tends to zero because the original row laws bound these sums and $\\mu_n\\to0$. The column calculation is identical. This proves both centered-truncation diagonal laws explicitly.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-5",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "Enumerate the cells of the infinite quadrant by successive square shells $\\max(i,j)=k$, in fixed lexicographic order on each finite shell. The resulting sequence of $|\\xi_{ij}|^4$ is iid and integrable; F3 at the square cardinalities $k^2$ gives $k^{-2}\\sum_{i,j\\le k}|\\xi_{ij}|^4\\to E|\\xi|^4$. Enclosing each rectangle in a square of side $\\lceil Dn\\rceil$ gives $N_n^{-2}\\sum_{i\\le R_n,j\\le C_n}|\\xi_{ij}|^4=O(1)$. The maximum-entry conclusion for the centered entries follows from step 1.1 and $|\\mu_n|/\\sqrt{N_n}\\to0$. Also $|\\xi-\\mu_n|^4\\le8(|\\xi|^4+|\\mu_n|^4)$, so eventual equality to the truncations and $R_nC_n/N_n^2=O(1)$ give the fourth-power bound for the centered entries.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-6",
          "step": "6.1",
          "claim": "For every fixed $q>2$, the square of the Frobenius norm of the indicated power matrix is bounded by $(\\max|x_{ij}^{(n)}|/\\sqrt{N_n})^{2q-4}N_n^{-2}\\sum|x_{ij}^{(n)}|^4$, which tends to zero by step 5.1. Multiplying its entries by signs does not change the Frobenius norm. The original-entry assertion follows from the same calculation. All conclusions hold on the intersection of the countably many probability-one events already specified.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 5.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
