    "lem-sample-covariance-spectrum-confinement": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-nested-iid-fourth-moment-truncation-and-diagonal-control",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\xi_{ij})_{i,j\\ge1}$ be one infinite iid real array with $E\\xi_{11}=0$, $E\\xi_{11}^2=1$ and $E|\\xi_{11}|^4<\\infty$. Consider any deterministic rectangular sequence $R_n\\times C_n$ with both $R_n/n$ and $C_n/n$ bounded above and bounded away from zero. Write $X_n=(\\xi_{ij})_{i\\le R_n,j\\le C_n}$ and $N_n=\\max(R_n,C_n)$. There is a deterministic sequence $\\delta_n\\to0$, which may be chosen to decrease more slowly than any specified finite list of rates tending to zero and to satisfy $\\delta_n\\ge1/\\log(n+2)$, such that, almost surely, $X_n$ equals its entrywise truncation at $\\delta_n\\sqrt{N_n}$ eventually. Set\n$$\\widehat\\xi_{ij}^{(n)}=\\xi_{ij}\\mathbf1_{\\{|\\xi_{ij}|\\le\\delta_n\\sqrt{N_n}\\}},\\quad\\mu_n=E\\widehat\\xi_{11}^{(n)},\\quad x_{ij}^{(n)}=\\widehat\\xi_{ij}^{(n)}-\\mu_n.$$\nThen $\\sqrt{R_nC_n/N_n}|\\mu_n|\\to0$, $E|x_{11}^{(n)}|^2\\le1$ tends to $1$, $|x_{ij}^{(n)}|\\le2\\delta_n\\sqrt{N_n}$ eventually, and $E|x_{11}^{(n)}|^q\\le K(2\\delta_n\\sqrt{N_n})^{q-3}$ for every integer $q\\ge3$, with $K$ independent of $n,q$. Almost surely the following controls hold for the original entries and for the centered truncated entries:\n$$\\max_{i\\le R_n}\\left|N_n^{-1}\\sum_{j\\le C_n}|x_{ij}^{(n)}|^2-C_n/N_n\\right|\\to0,\\qquad\\max_{j\\le C_n}\\left|N_n^{-1}\\sum_{i\\le R_n}|x_{ij}^{(n)}|^2-R_n/N_n\\right|\\to0,$$\n$$\\max_{i\\le R_n,j\\le C_n}|x_{ij}^{(n)}|/\\sqrt{N_n}\\to0,\\qquad N_n^{-2}\\sum_{i\\le R_n,j\\le C_n}|x_{ij}^{(n)}|^4=O(1).$$\nFor every fixed $q>2$, the rectangular matrix with entry $N_n^{-q/2}|x_{ij}^{(n)}|^q$ has Frobenius norm tending to zero; the assertion also holds with a sign attached to each entry.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1",
            "7.1",
            "8.1",
            "9.1",
            "10.1",
            "11.1",
            "13.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-nonbacktracking-covariance-high-moment-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\xi_{ij})_{i,j\\ge1}$ be one infinite real iid array with $E\\xi_{11}=0$, $E\\xi_{11}^2=1$ and $E|\\xi_{11}|^4<\\infty$, and let $p_h/h\\to\\gamma\\in(0,\\infty)$. Put $N_h=\\max(h,p_h)$, $m_h=\\min(h,p_h)$ and $y_h=m_h/N_h\\to y:=\\min(\\gamma,\\gamma^{-1})$. Take the centered slow truncations of the upper-left $h\\times p_h$ blocks, orient each with $m_h$ rows and $N_h$ columns, and divide by $\\sqrt{N_h}$, obtaining $Y_h$. For each fixed integer $\\ell\\ge1$, define $T_{h,\\ell}$ by summing the products of the entries of $Y_h,Y_h^{\\mathsf T},\\ldots,Y_h,Y_h^{\\mathsf T}$ along all paths of length $2\\ell$, subject to every pair of vertices two positions apart being unequal. Its row and column endpoints are included in these exclusions. Then almost surely\n$$\\limsup_h\\|T_{h,\\ell}\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le(2\\ell+1)(\\ell+1)y^{(\\ell-1)/2}.$$\nIn particular, for the original untruncated $X_h=(\\xi_{ij})_{i\\le h,j\\le p_h}$, almost surely $\\limsup_h\\|X_h/\\sqrt h\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}<\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1",
            "7.1",
            "8.1",
            "9.1",
            "10.1",
            "11.1",
            "12.1",
            "13.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space and let $T:V\\to V$ be\nself-adjoint. Then $V$ has an orthonormal basis consisting of eigenvectors of\n$T$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1",
            "7.1",
            "8.1",
            "9.1",
            "10.1",
            "11.1",
            "12.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-singular-value-decomposition",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $T:V\\to W$ be a linear map between finite-dimensional real or complex inner\nproduct spaces, and let $r$ be the number of positive singular values of $T$.\nThen there exist orthonormal bases $(e_1,\\dots,e_n)$ of $V$ and\n$(f_1,\\dots,f_m)$ of $W$, together with singular values\n\n$$s_1\\ge\\cdots\\ge s_r>0,$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$Tv=\\sum_{j=1}^r s_j\\langle v,e_j\\rangle f_j$$\n\nfor every $v\\in V$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1",
            "7.1",
            "8.1",
            "9.1",
            "10.1",
            "11.1",
            "12.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Orient the upper-left block or its transpose with $m_h$ rows and $N_h$ columns, and use F1 to center and truncate it. In the rest of the proof write $n=N_h$, $p=m_h$, $y_h=p/n\\to y\\in(0,1]$, and $Y$ for this centered normalized matrix. F2 gives every fixed-length bound for $T_j$, in particular $\\|Y\\|=O(1)$. The diagonal laws give $D=\\operatorname{diag}(YY^{\\mathsf T})\\to I$ and $E=\\operatorname{diag}(Y^{\\mathsf T}Y)\\to yI$ in operator norm. Set $T=T_1$ and $T_0=I$. All limits below are along the original sequence index $h$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-2",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "For compatible alternating rectangular matrices, write $A_1\\odot\\cdots\\odot A_s$ for the path sum imposing $i_r\\ne i_{r+2}$ at every available position (including endpoints). Its norm is at most $2^{s-1}\\prod\\|A_i\\|$. Here is a local proof rather than an appeal to Appendix A. Expand at the first forbidden reversal: $$A_1\\odot\\cdots\\odot A_s=A_1(A_2\\odot\\cdots\\odot A_s)-\\operatorname{diag}(A_1A_2)(A_3\\odot\\cdots\\odot A_s)+(A_1\\circ A_2^{\\mathsf T}\\circ A_3)\\odot A_4\\odot\\cdots\\odot A_s.$$",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-3",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "The correction restores the additional exclusion lost when the first reversal is contracted. This identity follows by fixing the indices $i_1=i_3$, then excluding $i_2=i_4$; for $s=2$ it is simply deletion of the diagonal. Rectangular diagonal matrices have the compatible rectangular identity embedding where necessary. The diagonal term has norm at most $\\|A_1\\|\\|A_2\\|$. The fused Schur product has norm at most $\\|A_1\\|\\|A_2\\|\\|A_3\\|$: it is a compression of $A_1\\otimes A_2^{\\mathsf T}\\otimes A_3$ by the coordinate isometries $e_a\\mapsto e_a\\otimes e_a\\otimes e_a$ and the analogous column isometry. The tensor-product norm bound follows by factoring a tensor operator into maps acting on one coordinate at a time; each such map is an orthogonal direct sum of copies of that factor and therefore has norm at most its norm, by summing the squared block norms. With $c_1=1$, $c_2=2$, induction gives $c_s\\le c_{s-1}+2c_{s-2}\\le2^{s-1}$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-4",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "Define $Y^{[q]}$ to have entries $n^{-q/2}|x|^{q-1}x$ for odd $q$ and $n^{-q/2}|x|^q$ for even $q$. The strong-law inputs give $\\|Y^{[2]}\\|_F^2=O(1)$ and $\\|Y^{[q]}\\|_F^2\\le(\\max|x|/\\sqrt n)^{2q-4}n^{-2}\\sum|x|^4\\to0$ for $q>2$. By F2, $\\|Y\\|^2\\le\\|T\\|+\\|\\operatorname{diag}(YY^*)\\|=O(1)$. Thus every fixed-length restricted product with at least one factor $Y^{[q]}$, $q>2$, is $o(1)$ in norm.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-5",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "Centering comparison is now legitimate without circularity: F2 proves the graph estimate for centered truncations and bounds their $\\|Y\\|=O(1)$ using their row law; the mean matrix has norm $\\sqrt p\\,|E x_{11}^{\\mathrm{tr}}|=o(1)$ by F1. Expand the difference of fixed-length restricted products multilinearly and apply the norm inequality. There are $2^{2j}-1$ terms, not the book's printed $2j-1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-6",
          "step": "6.1",
          "claim": "Let $D=\\operatorname{diag}(YY^*)\\to I$, $E=\\operatorname{diag}(Y^*Y)\\to yI$ in norm. Let $B_j$ be the restricted product of length $2j-1$ starting with $Y^*$ and alternating thereafter. For $j\\ge2$, the deterministic restricted-product identity above gives $$T_j=YB_j-DT_{j-1}+Y^{[3]}\\odot Y^*\\odot\\cdots\\odot Y^*=YB_j-T_{j-1}+o(1).$$",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "step 5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-7",
          "step": "7.1",
          "claim": "For $j=1$, $YB_1=T_1+D=T_1+T_0+o(1)$, so the unified relation is $YB_j=T_j+T_{j-1}+o(1)$ for all $j\\ge1$. Similarly $$B_{j+1}=Y^*T_j-EB_j+(Y^{[3]})^*\\odot Y\\odot\\cdots\\odot Y^*.$$",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "step 6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-8",
          "step": "8.1",
          "claim": "For $j=1$ the final correction is simply $(Y^{[3]})^*$, which is $o(1)$; this case must not be silently omitted. All restricted products have fixed length and bounded norm; the fused corrections vanish by the estimates above. Therefore $$T_{j+1}=YB_{j+1}-DT_j+o(1)=(YY^*-D)T_j-yYB_j+o(1)=TT_j-yT_j-yT_{j-1}+o(1).$$",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "step 7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-9",
          "step": "9.1",
          "claim": "Errors are in operator norm, for each fixed $j$. The recurrence holds with $T_0=I$ and includes $j=1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "step 8.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-10",
          "step": "10.1",
          "claim": "Put $H=T-yI$. Define $P_0(z)=1$, $P_1(z)=z+y$, and $P_{j+1}(z)=zP_j(z)-yP_{j-1}(z)$. Since $\\|H\\|=O(1)$, induction in the recurrence gives $T_j=P_j(H)+o(1)$ for each fixed $j$. Explicitly $$P_j(z)=y^{j/2}\\left[U_j\\left(\\frac z{2\\sqrt y}\\right)+\\sqrt y\\,U_{j-1}\\left(\\frac z{2\\sqrt y}\\right)\\right],$$ where $U_{-1}=0$, $U_0=1$, $U_{j+1}(t)=2tU_j(t)-U_{j-1}(t)$. This explicit formula follows immediately by checking the two initial values and recurrence.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "step 9.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-11",
          "step": "11.1",
          "claim": "For real $|t|\\ge1+\\epsilon$, write $|t|=\\cosh a$ and $\\rho=e^a>1$. The recurrence directly gives $U_j(\\cosh a)=\\sinh((j+1)a)/\\sinh a$ and $U_j(-t)=(-1)^jU_j(t)$. Hence $|U_j(t)|\\ge\\rho^j$ and $|U_{j-1}(t)|/|U_j(t)|\\le\\rho^{-1}$, the last inequality following from $\\sinh(ja)/\\sinh((j+1)a)<e^{-a}$. Therefore $|U_j(t)+\\sqrt yU_{j-1}(t)|\\ge(1-\\sqrt y/\\rho)\\rho^j\\ge(1-\\rho^{-1})\\rho^j$. This is strictly positive uniformly for $|t|\\ge1+\\epsilon$, including $y=1$. If $\\|H\\|>2\\sqrt y+\\epsilon$ infinitely often, the finite-dimensional spectral theorem and spectral mapping for a polynomial give, for every fixed $j$, $y^{j/2}(1-\\rho_\\epsilon^{-1})\\rho_\\epsilon^j\\le\\limsup\\|P_j(H)\\|\\le(2j+1)(j+1)y^{(j-1)/2}$ with a fixed $\\rho_\\epsilon>1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "step 10.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-12",
          "step": "12.1",
          "claim": "The inequality in step 11.1 is impossible for all positive integers $j$: after dividing by $y^{j/2}$, its left side grows exponentially in $j$ while the right side is the polynomial $(2j+1)(j+1)y^{-1/2}$. Thus almost surely $\\limsup_h\\|H\\|\\le2\\sqrt y$. Since $YY^{\\mathsf T}-(1+y)I-H=D-I\\to0$, the centered matrices satisfy the stated confinement. All fixed-$j$ estimates hold simultaneously by intersecting countably many probability-one events.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 10.1",
            "step 11.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-derivation-13",
          "step": "13.1",
          "claim": "Let $A_h$ denote the original oriented upper-left block divided by $\\sqrt{N_h}$. F1 gives $\\|A_h-Y_h\\|\\to0$ through eventual truncation equality and the explicit constant-mean norm, while F2 gives $\\|Y_h\\|=O(1)$ and hence $\\|A_h\\|=O(1)$. Thus $\\|A_hA_h^{\\mathsf T}-Y_hY_h^{\\mathsf T}\\|\\le(\\|A_h\\|+\\|Y_h\\|)\\|A_h-Y_h\\|\\to0$. The proved centered confinement therefore passes to the original matrices. Since the argument allowed $y_h=1$ and orientation changes, it includes the square-aspect boundary.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra",
            "step 12.1"
          ]
        }
